ws2_32 answers the machine's own name from its interface addresses, so a game that looks itself up never waits on the local network.

This repository's patch to ws2_32. It extends to getaddrinfo what Wine's gethostbyname already does for the
machine's own name (get_local_ips: the local addresses, with no lookup), and it is what Windows does: a Windows
machine answers its own name from its own adapters. Wine bug 60322 (fixed in Wine 11.19, Hans Leidekker) and bug
52133 (Wine 6.23, the same route for WPAD) are the same family of fault: a .local name on macOS goes to multicast
DNS. No upstream or downstream code answers getaddrinfo's own name this way, so nothing is copied.

The fault: Unreal's GetLocalHostAddr and other games call getaddrinfo or gethostbyname on gethostname()'s answer,
which macOS makes "<LocalHostName>.local". Only multicast DNS answers a .local name, and since 0037 every Windows
program runs from the engine's mavrik.app, so macOS holds that query behind its Local Network privacy prompt
("Allow "mavrik" to find devices on local networks?"). While the prompt was unanswered the lookup waited 35 s and
failed (WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND); 0041 removed the same lookup from wineboot.

The own name is now matched in any case: gethostname()'s answer, its first label, that label in .local, or the
NetBIOS computer name (the label's first 15 characters). getaddrinfo (and with it GetAddrInfoW and GetAddrInfoExW)
answers it in the Unix library from getifaddrs: non-loopback IPv4 first, then non-loopback IPv6 (without the
link-local addresses of tunnels), then loopback, each with the socket types, protocols and port that a lookup of no
host gives, and the host name as the canonical name on every entry when asked (as the system's own answer has
it). AI_NUMERICHOST is left to the system as before. gethostbyname uses the same match for its existing
local-address answer instead of an exact one. Every other name is looked up as before, so a game that really looks
for other machines on the local network still asks.

Checked (mavrik S217 lab, display off, fresh prefixes, interleaved, twice each, the engine bundle's prompt
unanswered): applies after patches 0001 to 0041 with no fuzz; ws2_32 builds with the engine's toolchain with no new
warning. In the engine bundle every own-name call (getaddrinfo on the host name, its first label, the NetBIOS name and
the name in capitals, GetAddrInfoW, gethostbyname) answered in 0.0 s with the Mac's addresses, where without this
patch getaddrinfo and GetAddrInfoW on the host name each waited 35.0 s and failed. The control without the bundle
answered the host name the same way through multicast DNS. A lookup of another machine's .local name still waits for
the player's answer (35.0 s while it is unanswered), and fails at once after "Don't Allow".

diff --git a/dlls/ws2_32/ws2_32_private.h b/dlls/ws2_32/ws2_32_private.h
index 9a4d079..0d0c49c 100644
--- a/dlls/ws2_32/ws2_32_private.h
+++ b/dlls/ws2_32/ws2_32_private.h
@@ -76,6 +76,20 @@ static inline char *strdupWtoA( const WCHAR *str )
 
 static const char magic_loopback_addr[] = {127, 12, 34, 56};
 
+/* Is NAME the machine's own host name HOST (gethostname()'s answer), in any case: HOST itself, its first label,
+ * that label in .local, or the NetBIOS computer name (the first label's first 15 characters)? */
+static inline BOOL is_own_host_name( const char *name, const char *host )
+{
+    const char *dot = strchr( host, '.' );
+    size_t len = dot ? dot - host : strlen( host );
+
+    if (!name || !name[0]) return FALSE;
+    if (!strcasecmp( name, host )) return TRUE;
+    if (len > 15 && strlen( name ) == 15) len = 15;
+    if (strncasecmp( name, host, len )) return FALSE;
+    return !name[len] || !strcasecmp( name + len, ".local" );
+}
